  "prediction": "**War-Related and International Emergency Funding: A Review of Congressional Oversight Issues**\n\nSince the 9/11 terrorist attacks, Congress has appropriated approximately $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority for war-related and international emergency or contingency-designated activities. This report provides background information and potential oversight issues for Congress on this funding, which has been designated for emergencies or Overseas Contingency Operations/Global War on Terrorism (OCO/GWOT).\n\n**Funding Breakdown**\n\nThe majority of this funding, $1.8 trillion, has been allocated to the Department of Defense (DOD), with $154 billion going to the Department of State and U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID), and $3 billion to the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Coast Guard.\n\n**OCO/GWOT Designation**\n\nThe OCO/GWOT designation was created to exempt funding from statutory discretionary spending limits, allowing for more flexibility in responding to unforeseen needs. However, this designation has been criticized for being ambiguous and has been used to fund activities unrelated to contingency operations.\n\n**Budgetary Implications**\n\nThe reliance on OCO funding has created uncertainty in defense planning and has contributed to larger deficits. Critics argue that OCO has become a \"slush fund\" for military and foreign affairs spending unrelated to contingency operations.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report concludes that Congress should consider transitioning longer-term OCO expenses to the base budget, which could be achieved through a combination of increased budget caps, targeted cuts in inefficient Defense programs, and increased revenues. This would provide a more stable and predictable funding source for national security needs.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority has been allocated for war-related and international emergency or contingency-designated activities since 9/11.\n* The majority of this funding has been allocated to the Department of Defense.\n* The OCO/GWOT designation has been criticized for being ambiguous and has been used to fund activities unrelated to contingency operations.\n* The reliance on OCO funding has created uncertainty in defense planning and has contributed to larger deficits.\n* Congress should consider transitioning longer-term OCO expenses to the base budget to provide a more stable and predictable funding source for national security needs.",
